  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n{\u222e}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\n \nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in ${R}^{2},$ which of the following could be the functions $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)?$\n\n- $P(x,y)=y$ and $Q(x,y)=-x$\n- $P(x,y)=\\frac{x}{2}+\\operatorname{cos}\u2061{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=\\frac{y}{2}$\n- $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x$",
  "worked_solution": "According to Green's theorem, if \n\n$$\n\\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202x}-\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202y}=1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={\u222c}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\u222c}_{D}(\\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202x}-\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\u222e}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Pair I does *not* give the required area. In this case, \n$\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202y}=1$\nand\n$\\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202x}=-1,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202x}-\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202y}=-2\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Pair II does *not* give the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202y}=-2y\\operatorname{sin}\u2061{y}^{2}$\nand\n$\\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202x}=0,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202x}-\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202y}=2y\\operatorname{sin}\u2061{y}^{2}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Pair III gives the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202y}=0$\nand\n$\\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202x}=1,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202x}-\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202y}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
